[Music]  welcome back to cbs this morning the  federal government's newest jobs report  out this morning reveals a slowing  recovery from the impact of the  coronavirus  the labor department says the u.s  economy added nearly 1.8 million  jobs in july it did beat expectations  from 1.6  the unemployment rate fell to 10.2  percent  but that is down from the june numbers  cbs news business analyst jill  schlesinger  joins us now joe what do you make of  these numbers so far  well i mean these numbers were very  difficult to predict  so here's what you want to remember that  the virus  surge in the south and the west caused  a lot of municipalities to change their  rules they were slowing down their  reopening process  and of course consumers and businesses  in those places  also had to adjust and this meant that  the pace of job growth really did  slow down remember back in the beginning  of this  virus march and april 22 million jobs  lost  then we saw in may and june  seven and a half million jobs created  which is fantastic  now we're slowing down a bit it's  probably because of the spread of this  virus  the unemployment rate coming down is  certainly a good sign but i also like to  look at  the broader unemployment rate it  captures a lot of people who are working  part-time when they really like  full-time work  that rate now is down at about 16 and a  half percent  now again that's a higher number but it  peaked at about 22-23  back in the spring so directionally  things are in the right direction  it's just the pace of improvement has  slowed down and jill how much of this  is the economy that was due for a  recession versus pandemic  well this is clearly pandemic related i  mean there is no doubt that when you see  22 million jobs vaporize over the course  of two months  that that is because the entire nation  shut down  however i think the reopening process  is starting to actually weed out a lot  of different businesses  so businesses who came into the period  very shaky  you're going to see them to their you're  going to see their failures  you're going to see big department  stores who maybe have a lot of debt  they're not going to make it you may see  mom and pop  restaurants that were operating on razor  thin margins  they may not make it so in fact the  pandemic's probably an accelerant  to what was going on previously it's  been a lot of talk about the  the uh the debate over the new uh  potential new stimulus package here  uh joe which has still not happened or  no agreement has been made  and a lot of talk about whether the  larger checks or the extra checks that  are going to people are discouraging  them from going back to work there is  some new research out on that what is it  what does it tell you  we actually see no correlation between  higher unemployment benefits and people  looking for work  in fact it's quite the opposite when  people are making more money  they have time to sit down look through  their options and actually  seek employment and there is a study  that's out from yale that confirms this  we see some research from the federal  reserve banks there's also an  interesting paper just out from the  national  bureau of economic research and they  were looking back  at the great recession of 2008 and 2009  when benefits were not just bigger but  extended for a longer period of time  and that paper found something really  very critical  and that is when people have a longer  period of time to look for work and the  security of unemployment benefits  they find a better job for them their  skills match the job that's out there  that's important because we actually  don't want people taking jobs  that may be below their skill level  because that takes the job away from  somebody at that skill level  so in fact richer and longer  unemployment benefits  may be the key to recovering in the  labor market more quickly  all right and jill so it also worth the  new york fed  reported that household debt in the  middle of all this household that  actually decreased in the second quarter  can you talk about that  well this had a lot to do with credit  card debt and credit card debt shrunk  for the first time  in six years and the largest decline in  seven years that has a lot to do with  people just not wanting to spend money  but also remember those twelve hundred  dollar stimulus checks a lot of people  use that money to pay down outstanding  debt  look as a certified financial planner  that's great economists don't love this  they want to see people spend more  freely  obviously as the economy starts to  loosen up and return to more normal  levels  many think that these debt levels will  rise once again jeff  okay jill schlesinger as always thank  you very much  take care

